{"id":"u-daf8f8d9-d02f-4370-8353-c3b89580d417","src":"community","title":"Classic Aesthetics","author":"Bryce Rogers","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/mJxsbJ-arms-and-chest-some-delt","desc":"Built on the Silver and Golden Era idea that a physique should be judged by shape, not just size. The target is the Steve Reeves look: wide shoulders, a back that flares from a small waist, and arms, chest, and legs that match instead of one body part standing out. Frank Zane's focus on proportion and clean lines is the other big influence, and the training intensity comes from Dorian Yates: working sets go close to failure, and the isolation work goes all the way.\n\nThis is a 6-day program for intermediate lifters. It isn't for beginners, who will grow on far less, and it isn't meant for advanced lifters either. You need a couple of years of consistent training, a good feel for RPE, and the food and sleep to recover from six days a week.\n\nThe split is Upper A, Lower A, and Shoulders and Arms A, then the same three days again with different exercises (B). Back and shoulder work gets the most attention, because shoulder-to-waist ratio is what creates the taper, while chest, arms, and legs are trained in proportion instead of specialized. That's about 100 working sets a week, a maximum of 3 sets per exercise, and no supersets.\n\nMain lifts (squat, barbell row, presses) stay at RPE 8-9. Everything else runs RPE 9-10. Progression is double progression: when every set hits the top of its rep range, add weight next session. It runs in 6-week blocks with a deload in week 6 (same weights, capped RPE of 7-8).\n\nBest run at maintenance or in a surplus, with 7+ hours of sleep.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":80,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-19T15:01:18.376Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Upper A","Lower A","Shoulders + Arms A","Upper B","Lower B","Shoulders + Arms B"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"